# Line endings are git's problem, not the editor's.
#
# Every file here was committed with CRLF in the blob itself, so any tool that
# rewrote one with LF turned a small edit into a whole-file diff and buried the
# actual change. Normalising means git stores LF and hands out CRLF on checkout,
# so what a tool writes stops mattering and endings stop appearing in reviews.
#
# `eol=crlf` rather than leaving it to each machine's `core.autocrlf`: this
# drives Windows panels and is built and run on Windows, so there is one right
# answer and no reason to make it a per-clone setting that can be wrong.
* text=auto eol=crlf

# Marked rather than left to detection, which only reads the first 8000 bytes.
# A misdetected icon is corrupted on checkout, not merely noisy.
*.png binary
*.ico binary

# The Windows script that launches the daemon. WSH is fussy about its input, so
# this one is CRLF because it has to be, not because of the rule above.
*.vbs text eol=crlf
